    "case_id": "95",
    "case_summary": "A 74-year-old Afro-Caribbean man presented with a 4-week history of obstructive jaundice, weight loss, and fatigue. He had a history of noninsulin-dependent diabetes and hypertension. Nineteen months earlier, he experienced polyuria and nocturia, leading to findings of impaired renal function and poorly perfused kidneys on CT scan, with retroperitoneal fibrosis obstructing both ureters. Bilateral ureteric stents were subsequently inserted. The patient remained well until the recent onset of jaundice. Ultrasound showed extrahepatic and intrahepatic biliary dilatation without gallstones. The pancreas appeared bulky throughout, with no focal lesion identified. ERCP revealed an irregular stricture in the lower common bile duct, and an endobiliary stent was placed. Segments of the pancreatic duct were also dilated. CT confirmed a markedly bulky pancreas with no focal mass lesion or abnormal calcifications. There was thickening of soft tissue around the porta hepatis, suggestive of lymphadenopathy. An autoimmune screen was negative for ANA, cytoplasmic antibodies (Hep-2), ANCA IIF, mitochondrial antibodies, smooth muscle antibodies, liver/kidney microsomal antibodies, Anti-GPC, rheumatoid factor, and striated muscle antibodies. The IgG level was elevated at 27.0 g/L (5.3–16.5 g/L), particularly IgG4 at 15.10 g/L (0.0–1.3 g/L).\n\nAt laparotomy, the entire pancreas was hard, swollen, and actively inflamed, most particularly the body of the gland. A shave biopsy of a nodule on the body of pancreas plus three core biopsies with a Trucut needle and a fourth from the head of pancreas were submitted to frozen-section histopathology examination; all showed florid chronic inflammation but no cancer. A portion of thickened retroperitoneum tissue in front of the aorta was also removed and this was shown to be benign fibrotic tissue.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
